Similarly for destination.

Thus we need to prepare origin_availability_alpha and destination_availability_alpha, link task profile, activate nodes, assign operator, lock them.

We also need to assign operator_resource to them (assign_operator_resource). That will consume operator_available, making it unavailable. But goal requires operator_available at end. We could later release the operator via release_operator_resource after we are done using it. Since we need operator for both origin and destination lock actions, we could assign operator to origin_availability, lock it, then release operator, then assign to destination, lock, release. However lock_origin_availability does not consume operator; it only requires candidate_operator_linked (i.e., operator assigned). It does not release it. So after lock, operator remains linked. To free operator we need to release_operator_resource. That will make operator_available again and remove candidate_operator_linked.

Thus we can assign operator to origin, lock, release operator (making it available again), assign to destination, lock, release. At the end operator_available will be true.

Alternatively we could assign operator to both simultaneously? Not possible because operator can only be linked to one candidate at a time (since candidate_operator_linked is a predicate; if we try to assign to another while already linked, preconditions require operator_available, which would be false). So we need to release between.

Now also need candidate_prepared for origin_availability_alpha and destination_availability_alpha. Candidate_prepared requires:
- candidate_created ?candidate
